WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:01.001
This has an

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.602
immediate effect on everything, right?

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.637
You owe our lives everything.

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:07.107
Just in the US Bureau of Labor Statistics

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:08.308
just announcing

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:09.376
how many jobs were added

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:10.910
to the economy in February.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.345
Christine Romans here with the numbers.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.546
So a lot of hiring.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:14.581
This is good or bad.

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:16.816
If you are looking for a job, it's good.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:18.418
If you have a job and you're job hopping,

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:19.486
it's good.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.421
It is a strong job market.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:23.556
311,000 jobs added.

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:25.558
And we know it was a blockbuster report

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:26.159
in January

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:27.060
that was revised down

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.229
a little bit to 504,000.

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:32.098
That's still a very, very big number.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:36.069
This shows you that businesses are hiring

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:37.170
despite those headlines

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.671
you've seen of tech companies

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.874
that have been laying people off.

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:42.976
They hired a lot during the pandemic.

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:44.244
Despite those headlines,

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.279
this is a very, very strong job market.

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:48.281
3.6% is the unemployment

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:49.382
rate went up a little bit.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.583
That's because

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.052
about 400,000 people

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:52.986
came off the sidelines

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:54.354
and started looking for work.

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:55.622
So that's an unemployment rate

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:56.790
rising for a good reason.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.925
People are hearing from friends

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:01.227
they know that there are plentiful jobs

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:03.630
and so they're out looking for work now.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:07.100
And then the sectors, guys, the leisure,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.101
hospitality, hiring

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.437
a lot of people retail, government jobs.

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:13.440
So we broke out for a lot of

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:14.941
just a lot of jobs across the board.

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:16.409
So it's good for employees,

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.179
bad for employers, bad for the Fed. Yeah.

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:22.348
So a good economy

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:23.850
could eventually be bad news

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:24.617
for Americans

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:26.019
because it means the Fed

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.054
has to continue to raise interest rates.

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.924
You've got wages up 4.6%.

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:31.825
That's great.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:32.292
I mean, people's

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:34.627
paychecks are unequivocally bigger.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:35.528
But it's

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:37.230
feeding into the inflation story.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.165
And so the Fed thinks that inflation

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:41.468
staying too high is more dangerous

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:42.368
than maybe

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.703
trying to cool off the job market

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:44.237
a little bit.

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:46.940
So I know it's upside down.

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:47.640
Well, it's weird

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.075
because everybody I talked to

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:49.442
and they said

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.043
just yesterday

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:50.910
I was having breakfast

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.278
with a bunch of folks and they said,

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:53.213
Don, we're so confused.

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:54.881
When you guys talk about the economy,

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:55.715
you get the new jobs

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:56.716
numbers in the stock market.

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:58.284
Everything's going gangbusters.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.619
But again, it's bad.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:01.321
What do I don't know what to say to them.

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:03.022
You know, it's it's interesting

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.191
because we kind of broke all the

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:07.160
the models after COVID.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:07.894
And so we're trying

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:08.995
we have a very confusing picture

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:09.863
of the economy.

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:11.331
The underlying strength of the economy

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:11.731
over the past

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.166
six weeks has been undeniable.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:14.534
The consumer

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:15.768
retail sales,

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:17.704
the job market and inflation

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:18.471
is still too high.

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:19.772
All of this is a function

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.941
of us, of a strong recovery

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:22.909
in the economy.

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:24.144
And while that is good news,

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:25.145
it feels like good news.

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:26.613
If it feeds into inflation

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:27.580
and inflation

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:28.815
gets entrenched,

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.183
then that slows growth

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:31.484
and then that can cause a recession.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.486
And the question

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:34.020
everyone is paying

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:34.888
close attention to this,

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:36.556
not just us, it's the White House,

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:37.557
our senior White House correspondent

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:38.825
and daily is on the line.

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:39.692
M.J., obviously,

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:42.328
they are always looking closely to this.

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:43.129
You reported

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:45.165
they were looking for this Goldilocks

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:47.467
number, got a lot higher than Goldilocks.

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.002
It's way more than expected.

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:50.003
What's the reaction

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:51.504
you're hearing so far?

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:52.472
Yeah, Caitlin,

00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:54.240
the number that White House officials

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:54.774
had really been

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:55.475
hoping to see

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:57.410
was something in the mid 200.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:57.977
So you're right

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.379
that this is a little bit

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:00.146
bigger than that.

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:01.781
But I can say I suspect

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:02.916
that they're going to be pretty

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:04.017
pleased with this.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:05.051
You know, that Goldilocks

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:06.653
number that we talk about

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:07.353
they wanted something

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:08.955
that wasn't too big like like what

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:10.557
we saw in the month of January

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:11.291
when the economy

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:13.593
added more than 500,000 jobs.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.761
That was frankly shocking

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.095
to a lot of economists.

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:17.530
But they obviously also

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.299
always want to see

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:20.900
strength and robustness

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.168
in the jobs market.

00:03:22.168 --> 00:03:24.037
And so much of this has to do

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:26.306
with being on federal reserve watch.

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:27.173
They knew

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:28.708
and very much took note

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:29.442
inside the building

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:30.243
behind me

00:03:30.243 --> 00:03:30.810
when Jerome

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.477
Powell said

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.513
in recent days and suggested

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:34.881
that if there are more

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:36.015
indicators of the jobs

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:37.283
market is just too hot,

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:39.052
that the labor market is too hot,

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:39.519
that, yeah,

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:40.253
the central bank

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:41.254
could bring back

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:43.623
more aggressive interest rate hikes.

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:44.857
That's not something that the White House

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:45.658
wants to see.

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:46.559
But at the same time,

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:47.660
this is almost sort of been

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.661
a good problem

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.263
for the White House to have.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.899
You know, they know that the labor market

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:55.101
being too strong and being too hot

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:56.769
can lead to more aggressive

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:57.537
interest rate hikes.

00:03:57.537 --> 00:03:58.705
But at the same time,

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:00.406
they're always going to say, look,

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.475
more jobs being added, more paychecks

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:03.843
going out to Americans.

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:05.578
That is essentially a good thing.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:06.145
And, you know,

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.380
I don't have to tell you

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.715
the issue of the economy,

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:10.450
the issue of inflation.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.218
Those are some of the most important

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:14.120
political issues for this White House.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.756
And for Democrats heading into 20, 24.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.758
So they're always going to sort of root

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:20.260
for strength in the jobs market.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:21.694
And they do basically feel like

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:22.729
they have a good story

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:24.297
to tell on the economy.

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:24.764
But again,

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.299
inflation is the one

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:28.268
sort of intractable problem

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:29.502
that this president has faced.

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:30.303
And they very much

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:31.871
want to see that trend

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:34.140
to continue in a downward manner.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.908
Yeah, we'll see what President Biden says

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:36.643
when he speaks out.

00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:37.644
MJ, Christine,

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:37.944
thank you

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:38.611
both so much

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:40.680
for breaking down those massive,

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.649
very hot jobs numbers today.

00:04:42.649 --> 00:04:43.449
CNN Economics

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:44.651
and political commentator Catherine

00:04:44.651 --> 00:04:46.019
Rampell joining us this morning.

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:47.920
So here's what I think.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.621
And I don't think I'm

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:49.622
the only one, right?

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:50.890
I think a number of viewers and Americans

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.292
in general struggle with as well.

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:53.993
So we have this strong jobs

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:54.627
report, right?

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:57.463
So the jobs market is still hot.

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:58.898
Looking at some of those sectors,

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:00.366
you had pretty significant gains

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:02.435
in leisure and hospitality and in retail.

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.137
So people are needed in those sectors,

00:05:04.137 --> 00:05:05.705
which means people are spending money.

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:06.372
And we know the US

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:09.375
economy depends on consumer spending.

00:05:09.709 --> 00:05:12.078
Inflation is still too high, though.

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:13.479
So what are we supposed

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.148
to make of all of this

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:16.816
sort of a similar formula

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.951
that we've been seeing?

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.386
How do I put it all in perspective this

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:20.953
morning, Catherine?

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:24.757
It is legitimately confusing.

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:27.160
This is the 11th consecutive month

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.363
when forecasters have expected much lower

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:33.499
job growth than we got And it's weird.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.702
I mean, we've had eight rate

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:37.403
hikes, eight interest rate hikes.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:38.638
A lot of people were expecting,

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:40.039
if not a slowdown,

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:41.374
maybe even a recession.

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:42.942
But this recession is like

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:44.010
Waiting for Godot.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:44.344
You know,

00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:46.412
it's like people keep thinking

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.380
it's around the corner

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.548
and then it never arrives,

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:50.116
which is generally good news, right?

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:50.650
I mean, nobody's

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.352
really rooting for a recession.

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:53.786
But it is puzzling

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:56.689
how the economy has been able to maintain

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:57.290
its strength,

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.458
how the job market has been able

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:00.026
to maintain its strength.

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:01.294
Given these predictions,

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.329
and given the great stress

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:04.797
on the economy today.

00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:06.132
So you're not alone.

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:08.401
It's it's hard to make sense of.

00:06:08.401 --> 00:06:09.335
First of all, very good

00:06:09.335 --> 00:06:10.336
literary reference there.

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:11.771
Second of all,

00:06:11.771 --> 00:06:14.140
the Fed is trying this balance, right?

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:15.742
They listen,

00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:17.043
we should be celebrating

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:17.977
a strong job market.

00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:18.878
That means Americans

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:20.313
can find jobs and get paid.

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:21.981
By the way, the pay is going up as well.

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:22.648
But trouble

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.016
is, prices are still staying high.

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:25.752
The Fed and I understand

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:27.086
you and me and folks watching

00:06:27.086 --> 00:06:28.855
want to get those prices down.

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:30.690
Why aren't the interest rate

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.258
hikes working so far?

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:34.927
It's pretty aggressive ramp up.

00:06:34.927 --> 00:06:36.462
And how far do you see them

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:37.430
needing to go before

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:38.464
they begin to have an effect?

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.035
Again, it's a little bit unclear.

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:44.270
Is this about

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:45.872
there's just a longer lag

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:47.774
between the time when the Fed raises

00:06:47.774 --> 00:06:48.307
interest rates

00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:49.909
and it's really felt in the economy

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:51.110
or maybe we don't know

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:52.578
what the alternative universe

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:53.312
would have been.

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:53.579
Maybe

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:54.647
the economy would have been

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:56.749
even stronger, booming even more.

00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:58.985
Absent these rate hikes,

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:00.453
I also wonder to

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:01.988
what extent fiscal policy

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:04.157
is still playing a role in in

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:05.825
helping the economy expand,

00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:07.160
especially at the state level.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:08.261
Most states

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.897
have cut taxes in the past year.

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:12.799
Almost every state has cut taxes

00:07:12.799 --> 00:07:14.133
in the past two years.

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:15.401
So that's still giving people money

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:15.935
to spend.

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:17.703
That might be part of the explanation

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:18.304
here.

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:18.771
Know,

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:19.605
we also don't know

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.207
if if our headline numbers

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:23.009
are capturing all of the stress

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:24.010
that's out there.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:24.610
There might be something

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:25.745
we're missing in part because of

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:27.046
this weird economy.

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:28.581
You tweeted

00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:28.981
yesterday, I'm

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:29.649
paraphrasing here,

00:07:29.649 --> 00:07:30.216
but if I remember,

00:07:30.216 --> 00:07:31.517
it seems the effect of

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.753
no matter what the jobs report shows,

00:07:34.554 --> 00:07:36.422
the Fed is going to be mad.

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:39.725
How does the Fed deal with this latest

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:41.594
bit of data

00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:44.797
Well, what I said was

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:46.566
people will be mad at the Fed.

00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:48.968
Very sorry about that. Very challenging.

00:07:49.402 --> 00:07:51.504
That's okay. They're not that different.

00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:53.473
But I think the Fed is in

00:07:53.473 --> 00:07:54.707
a really challenging position

00:07:54.707 --> 00:07:56.008
because we have these

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:57.210
contradictory signals,

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.946
strong jobs market,

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:02.148
some stress in the financial sector.

00:08:02.148 --> 00:08:05.117
Inflation too high, maybe send signals

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:06.152
that some of those pressures

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:07.186
might be coming down,

00:08:07.186 --> 00:08:08.254
but then they reverse themselves.

00:08:08.254 --> 00:08:09.422
So who knows?

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:11.123
I think the Fed is looking at this

00:08:11.123 --> 00:08:12.558
report and saying, hmm,

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:13.326
the job market

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:15.895
still looks stronger than we anticipated.

00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:16.662
There are

00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:20.433
some signs that monetary policy is doing

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:21.100
we want it to do.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.536
But it's not sufficient.

00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:24.170
Next week,

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.638
we will get new inflation data.

00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:26.706
I think that there's going to be

00:08:26.706 --> 00:08:28.941
a lot of attention paid to those data,

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:30.843
and that will affect what the Fed does.

00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:32.311
But I think we should anticipate

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:34.914
rate hikes continuing for a while.

00:08:34.914 --> 00:08:36.315
You said something interesting there.

00:08:36.315 --> 00:08:38.251
You said that part of the inflationary

00:08:38.251 --> 00:08:40.419
push here are tax cuts at the state level

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:41.087
because all that

00:08:41.087 --> 00:08:42.054
in the political environment,

00:08:42.054 --> 00:08:42.822
all the criticism

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:44.490
has been leveled at COVID

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:45.258
relief money

00:08:45.258 --> 00:08:46.659
going back a couple of years now.

00:08:46.659 --> 00:08:47.960
But but are you saying that there's

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:49.428
there's inflationary tax

00:08:49.428 --> 00:08:50.796
policy at the state level?

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:53.266
Yes.

00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:53.833
I mean, I don't know how

00:08:53.833 --> 00:08:54.901
big the magnitude is,

00:08:54.901 --> 00:08:56.602
but states have had these

00:08:56.602 --> 00:08:58.204
huge budget surpluses

00:08:58.204 --> 00:08:59.572
in the last few years.

00:08:59.572 --> 00:09:00.406
That's partly

00:09:00.406 --> 00:09:02.742
because the economy has been quite strong

00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:04.210
and they have strong tax revenues.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:04.877
It's also partly

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:05.845
because they got a lot of money

00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:06.812
from Congress.

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:08.648
So they have a lot of money to play with.

00:09:08.648 --> 00:09:10.716
And that money was supposed to be used,

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:11.584
of course,

00:09:11.584 --> 00:09:13.352
for making investments

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:15.555
in schools and various other things.

00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:17.156
And to some extent, it's

00:09:17.156 --> 00:09:18.824
been used to just hand it back

00:09:18.824 --> 00:09:20.359
over to taxpayers

00:09:20.359 --> 00:09:21.527
in the form of property rate

00:09:21.527 --> 00:09:23.996
cuts, income tax cuts, etc..

00:09:23.996 --> 00:09:26.365
And so we don't have a great sense

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:27.133
because there isn't like

00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:28.301
a central database,

00:09:28.301 --> 00:09:29.302
a central repository

00:09:29.302 --> 00:09:31.037
that tells us how that money

00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:32.772
is flowing through to taxpayers.

00:09:32.772 --> 00:09:34.640
But I think it's quite possible

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.242
that that's part of the story here.

00:09:36.242 --> 00:09:37.476
People have more money to play with.

00:09:38.578 --> 00:09:39.445
Catherine Rampell

00:09:39.445 --> 00:09:40.313
always good to talk to you.

00:09:40.313 --> 00:09:41.113
Appreciate it. Thanks.

